{"title":"KVPY","category":"Engineering","bank":"kvpy","subject":"Physics","chapter":"Wave Optics","count":22,"questions":[{"id":52540,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Wave Optics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A source simultaneously emitting light at two wavelengths <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>400</mn><mi>nm</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>800</mn><mi>nm</mi></math> is used in the Young's double slit experiment. If the intensity of light at the slit for each wavelength is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo></math> then the maximum intensity that can be observed at any point on the screen is","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>"],"answer":3,"solution":"At central maxima<br />\nDue to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>400</mn><mi>nm</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math><br />\nDue to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>800</mn><mi>nm</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo> </mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi></mi></math><br />\nTotal Intensity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2020 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da7578577f2eb23d4579c"},{"id":52541,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Wave Optics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Consider a plane parallel beam of light incident on a plano-cylindrical lens as shown below. Which of the following will you observe on a screen placed at the focal plane of the lens ?\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FQO9-zoQznPgM1MKVf4pjxJbCHlJdRZHNQdFf_KKjPpQ.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FQO9-zoQznPgM1MKVf4pjxJbCHlJdRZHNQdFf_KKjPpQ.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FQO9-zoQznPgM1MKVf4pjxJbCHlJdRZHNQdFf_KKjPpQ.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">","options":["The screen will be uniformly illuminated.","There will be a single bright spot on the screen.","There will be a single bright line on the screen parallel to the $x$-axis","There will be a single bright line on the screen parallel to the y-axis"],"answer":3,"solution":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252Fm3JIu9g2KVZBbY9vKIcFOZ_MoWY-YhSwdhq62gBrAZ4.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fm3JIu9g2KVZBbY9vKIcFOZ_MoWY-YhSwdhq62gBrAZ4.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fm3JIu9g2KVZBbY9vKIcFOZ_MoWY-YhSwdhq62gBrAZ4.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\nno deflection of light beam","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (19 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213db29beea046c422e"},{"id":52542,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Wave Optics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Two circularly shaped linear polarisers are placed coaxially. The transmission axis of the first polarizer is at $30^{\\circ}$ from the vertical while the second one is at $60^{\\circ}$, both in the clockwise sense. If an unpolarised beam of light of intensity $\\mathrm{I}=20 \\mathrm{~W} / \\mathrm{m}^{2}$ is incident on this pair of polarisers, then the intensities $\\mathrm{I}_{1}$ and $\\mathrm{I}_{2}$ transmitted by the first and the second polarisers, respectively, will be close to","options":["$\\mathrm{I}_{1}=10.0 \\mathrm{~W} / \\mathrm{m}^{2}$ and $\\mathrm{I}_{2}=7.5 \\mathrm{~W} / \\mathrm{m}^{2}$","$\\mathrm{I}_{1}=20.0 \\mathrm{~W} / \\mathrm{m}^{2}$ and $\\mathrm{I}_{2}=15 \\mathrm{~W} / \\mathrm{m}^{2}$","$\\mathrm{I}_{1}=10.0 \\mathrm{~W} / \\mathrm{m}^{2}$ and $\\mathrm{I}_{2}=8.6 \\mathrm{~W} / \\mathrm{m}^{2}$","$\\mathrm{I}_{1}=15.0 \\mathrm{~W} / \\mathrm{m}^{2}$ and $\\mathrm{I}_{2}=0.0 \\mathrm{~W} / \\mathrm{m}^{2}$"],"answer":0,"solution":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252Fd1UQ8lT6wwmzrqFP-q_hGdz8bCrChm24OPkh5x8BtLI.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fd1UQ8lT6wwmzrqFP-q_hGdz8bCrChm24OPkh5x8BtLI.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fd1UQ8lT6wwmzrqFP-q_hGdz8bCrChm24OPkh5x8BtLI.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\n$\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathrm{I}_{0} &=20 \\mathrm{~W} / \\mathrm{m}^{2} \\\\\n\\mathrm{I}_{1} &=\\frac{\\mathrm{I}_{0}}{2}=\\frac{20}{2}=10 \\mathrm{~W} / \\mathrm{m}^{2} \\\\\n\\mathrm{I}_{2} &=\\mathrm{I}_{1} \\cos ^{2} 30^{\\circ} \\\\\n&=10\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{2}=10 . \\frac{3}{4} \\\\\n&=7.5 \\mathrm{w} / \\mathrm{m}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (5 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213b3fd7b22daa7602e"},{"id":52543,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Wave Optics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"<p>A camera filled with a polarizer is placed on a mountain, in a manner to record only the reflected image of the sun from the surface of a sea as shown in the figure. If the sun rises at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>00</mn><mo> </mo><mi>AM</mi></math> and sets at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>00</mn><mo> </mo><mi>PM</mi></math> during the summer, then at what time in the afternoon will the recorded image have the lowest intensity, assuming there are no clouds and intensity of the sun at the sea surface is constant throughout the day? (Refractive index of water <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>33</mn></math>)</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252Fc6027d0c-00e9-41f5-84d7-3bc8232176d0-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fc6027d0c-00e9-41f5-84d7-3bc8232176d0-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fc6027d0c-00e9-41f5-84d7-3bc8232176d0-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 350px; height: 143px;\" /></p>","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>12</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>32</mn><mo> </mo><mi>PM</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>32</mn><mo> </mo><mi>PM</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>00</mn><mo> </mo><mi>PM</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>00</mn><mo> </mo><mi>PM</mi></math>"],"answer":1,"solution":"<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252F72a9759a-3a02-4ccc-9ecd-1dcfe28d82ee-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F72a9759a-3a02-4ccc-9ecd-1dcfe28d82ee-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F72a9759a-3a02-4ccc-9ecd-1dcfe28d82ee-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 265px; height: 153px;\" /></p>\n\n<p>Camera will receive minimum intensity when. Light will incident at Brewsters's angle.</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo><mi>tan</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>53</mn><mo>°</mo></math></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252Fb51ac515-9afa-4daa-8ace-79e06cfbbf5e-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fb51ac515-9afa-4daa-8ace-79e06cfbbf5e-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fb51ac515-9afa-4daa-8ace-79e06cfbbf5e-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 165px;\" /></p>\n\n<p>time taken by sun to go from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math></p>\n\n<p>will be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>12</mn><mi>hr</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>180</mn><mo>°</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>×</mo><mn>143</mn><mo>°</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>53</mn><mi>hr</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn><mi>hr</mi><mn>32</mn><mo> </mo><mi>min</mi><mo>.</mo></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo></math> time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mo> </mo><mi>AM</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>9</mn><mo> </mo><mi>hr</mi><mo> </mo><mn>32</mn><mo> </mo><mi>min</mi><mo>⇒</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>32</mn><mo> </mo><mi>PM</mi></math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2020 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da75783f259b1766201c1"},{"id":52544,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Wave Optics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Three circularly shaped linear polarisers are placed coaxially. The transmission axis of the first polariser is at $30^{\\circ}$, the second one is at $60^{\\circ}$ and the third at $90^{\\circ}$ to the vertical all in the clockwise sense. Each polariser additionally absorbs $10 \\%$ of the light. If a vertically polarised beam of light of intensity $\\mathrm{I}=100\\mathrm{W} / \\mathrm{m}^{2}$ is incident on this assembly of polarisers, then the final intensity of the transmitted light will be close to","options":["$10 \\mathrm{~W} / \\mathrm{m}^{2}$","$20 \\mathrm{~W} / \\mathrm{m}^{2}$","$30 \\mathrm{~W} / \\mathrm{m}^{2}$","$50 \\mathrm{~W} / \\mathrm{m}^{2}$"],"answer":2,"solution":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252F07DikOHUnrynA_8EYndL7d8beOw1UndUh2d9C0MV8r8.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F07DikOHUnrynA_8EYndL7d8beOw1UndUh2d9C0MV8r8.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F07DikOHUnrynA_8EYndL7d8beOw1UndUh2d9C0MV8r8.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\n$\\mathrm{I}_{1}=\\mathrm{I}_{0} \\times 0.9 \\cos ^{2} 30^{\\circ}=\\mathrm{I}_{0} \\times 0.9 \\times \\frac{3}{4}\\mathrm{I}_{2}=\\mathrm{I}_{1} \\times 0.9 \\cos ^{2} 30^{\\circ}=\\mathrm{I}_{1} \\times 0.9 \\times \\frac{3}{4}\\mathrm{I}_{3}=\\mathrm{I}_{2} \\times 0.9 \\cos ^{2} 30^{\\circ}=\\mathrm{I}_{2} \\times 0.9 \\times \\frac{3}{4}\\Rightarrow \\mathrm{I}_{3}=\\mathrm{I}_{0}(0.9)^{3}\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)^{3}\\mathrm{I}_{3}=30.75 \\mathrm{w} / \\mathrm{m}^{2}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (19 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2136b763ab5e0a13911"},{"id":52545,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Wave Optics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"In Young's double slit experiment, the amplitudes of the two waves incident on the two slits are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>A</mi></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math> is the maximum intensity, then the intensity at a spot on the screen, where the phase difference between the two interfering waves is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>ϕ</mi></math>.","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mi>cos</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mn>3</mn></mfrac><msup><mi>sin</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mn>9</mn></mfrac><mfenced><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>cos</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mn>9</mn></mfrac><mfenced><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>cos</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>"],"answer":2,"solution":"<p>Resultant intensity when two waves with phase difference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>ϕ</mi></math> interfere is</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msqrt><msqrt><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msqrt><mi>cos</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></math></p>\n\n<p>As, intensity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi><mo>∝</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo></math> </p>\n\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></math> constant and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> is amplitude.</p>\n\n<p>So, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>cos</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></math></p>\n\n<p>Here, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>A</mi></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mo> </mo><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>A</mi></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mfenced><mi>A</mi></mfenced><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>A</mi></mrow></mfenced><mi>cos</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mfenced><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>cos</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>........(i)</p>\n\n<p>Intensity is maximum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfenced><mo>,</mo></math></p>\n\n<p>when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>cos</mi><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mfenced><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>9</mn></math>.......(ii)</p>\n\n<p>So, resultant intensity,</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mfenced><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>cos</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></mfenced></math></p>\n\n<p>[From Eqs. (i) and (ii)]</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mn>9</mn></mfrac><mfenced><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>cos</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></mfenced></math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2018 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da6a9fefff251e07f592a"},{"id":52546,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Wave Optics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"<p>A student is jogging on a straight path with the speed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn><mo> </mo><mi>km</mi></math> per hour. Perpendicular to the path is kept a pipe with its opening <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> from the road (see figure). Diameter of the pipe is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>45</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> At the other end of the pipe is a speaker emitting sound of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1280</mn><mo> </mo><mi>Hz</mi></math> towards the opening of the pipes. As the student passes in front of the pipe, she hears the speaker sound for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>T</mi></math> seconds. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>T</mi></math> is in the range (take speed of sound, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>320</mn><mo> </mo><msup><mi>ms</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>):</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252F905cfb80-b769-42b5-8655-179bf4764309-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F905cfb80-b769-42b5-8655-179bf4764309-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F905cfb80-b769-42b5-8655-179bf4764309-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 173px;\" /></p>","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>12</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>12</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>18</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>18</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>22</mn></math>"],"answer":0,"solution":"<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252F1a07db4c-7238-43eb-a42a-77eb9f483fb2-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F1a07db4c-7238-43eb-a42a-77eb9f483fb2-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F1a07db4c-7238-43eb-a42a-77eb9f483fb2-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 213px;\" /></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>320</mn><mn>1280</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>25</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math></p>\n\n<p>Using concept of diffraction of wave</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>b</mi><mi>sin</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>22</mn><mi>λ</mi></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>sin</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>25</mn><mn>45</mn></mfrac><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>22</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>678</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>tan</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>93</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>tan</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>D</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>y</mi><mo>⇒</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>Dtan</mi><mi>θ</mi></math></p>\n\n<p>time to cross this region <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>D</mi><mi>tan</mi><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mtext> speed </mtext></mfrac></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>93</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>≃</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>9</mn><mi>sec</mi></math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2020 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da75ef2e021e21e0bb52d"},{"id":52547,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Wave Optics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"<p>A right-angle isosceles prism is held on the surface of a liquid composed of miscible solvents <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> of refractive index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>50</mn></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>130</mn><mo>,</mo></math> respectively. The refractive index of prism is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></math> and that of the liquid is given by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mi>L</mi></math><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></mfenced><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></math> where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></math> is the percentage of solvent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> in the liquid:-</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252Fa813c47a-bd50-4b6c-a03a-f6e8aba6c01b-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fa813c47a-bd50-4b6c-a03a-f6e8aba6c01b-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fa813c47a-bd50-4b6c-a03a-f6e8aba6c01b-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 377px; height: 183px;\" /></p>\n\n<p>If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></math> is the critical angle at prism-liquid interface, the plot which best represents the variation of the critical angle with the precentage of solvent is:</p>","options":["<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252Fb0d3ed43-2976-4274-b57b-d76c8c665310-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fb0d3ed43-2976-4274-b57b-d76c8c665310-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fb0d3ed43-2976-4274-b57b-d76c8c665310-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 205px; height: 188px;\" />","<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252Fc7a3de3f-f60d-4b5c-8b12-d7e1257be984-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fc7a3de3f-f60d-4b5c-8b12-d7e1257be984-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fc7a3de3f-f60d-4b5c-8b12-d7e1257be984-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 202px; height: 186px;\" />","<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252Ff8a36e82-2fcd-4415-8236-6093531363d5-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Ff8a36e82-2fcd-4415-8236-6093531363d5-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Ff8a36e82-2fcd-4415-8236-6093531363d5-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 202px; height: 184px;\" />","<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252Fed11cf3b-e351-4947-a0f8-1e00a9a894ff-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fed11cf3b-e351-4947-a0f8-1e00a9a894ff-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fed11cf3b-e351-4947-a0f8-1e00a9a894ff-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 183px;\" />"],"answer":0,"solution":"<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></msub><mi>sin</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi></msub><mi>sin</mi><mn>90</mn><mo>°</mo></math><br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfenced><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></msub></mfrac></mfenced></math><br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></msub></mrow></mfenced><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfrac></mfenced></math><br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>→</mo></math> Graph between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></math> will be curve of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></math></p>\n\n<p>Check for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo></math> to find most appropriate graph<br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfrac></mfenced></math><br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfenced><mfrac><mn>14</mn><mn>15</mn></mfrac></mfenced><mo>≃</mo><mn>69</mn><mo>°</mo></math><br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo></math> Correct option is (A)</p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2020 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da7555dc0e773bbdd32ed"},{"id":52548,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Wave Optics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A total solar eclipse is observed from the earth. At the same an observer on the moon views the earth. She is most likely to see (E denotes the earth)\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FW_Ny6i7HJxb9v1OzVEWYFsYbSBQ27ZgNuJWuS_ZK4jQ.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FW_Ny6i7HJxb9v1OzVEWYFsYbSBQ27ZgNuJWuS_ZK4jQ.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FW_Ny6i7HJxb9v1OzVEWYFsYbSBQ27ZgNuJWuS_ZK4jQ.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">\n<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FW_Ny6i7HJxb9v1OzVEWYFsYbSBQ27ZgNuJWuS_ZK4jQ.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">\n<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FW_Ny6i7HJxb9v1OzVEWYFsYbSBQ27ZgNuJWuS_ZK4jQ.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">","options":["$\\mathrm{a}$","b","$c$","$d$"],"answer":1,"solution":"Theoritical","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (5 Nov SA)","_marksId":"6174d213f3abd2645a650cf3","questionType":"singleCorrect","type":"singleCorrect"},{"id":52549,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Wave Optics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A beam of monoenergetic electrons, which have been accelerated from rest by a potential $\\mathrm{U}$, is used to form an interference pattern in a Young's Double Slit experiment. The electrons are now accelerated by potential $4 \\mathrm{U}$. Then the fringe width","options":["remains the same","is half the original fringe width","is twice the original fringe width","is one-fourth the original fringe width"],"answer":1,"solution":"$\\mathrm{eV}=\\frac{1}{2} \\mathrm{mv}^{2} \\mathrm{so}, \\mathrm{eV}=\\frac{p^{2}}{2 \\mathrm{~m}}$\nand\n\n\\lambda=\\frac{h}{p}, \\Rightarrow V \\propto \\frac{1}{\\lambda^{2}} \\text { and } \\beta=\\frac{n \\lambda D}{d}\n\nSo as potential U becomes $4 \\mathrm{U}$ then $\\beta$ becomes half","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2015 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213870b6c9ca895623c"},{"id":52550,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Wave Optics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A monochromatic light source $\\mathrm{S}$ of wavelength $440 \\mathrm{~nm}$ is placed slightly above a plane mirror $\\mathrm{M}$ as shown. Image of $\\mathrm{S}$ in $\\mathrm{M}$ can be used as a virtual source to produce interference fringes on the screen. The distance of source $S$ from $O$ is $20.0 \\mathrm{~cm}$, and the distance of screen from $\\mathrm{O}$ is $100.0 \\mathrm{~cm}$ (figure is not to scale). If the angle $\\theta=0.50 \\times 10^{-3}$ radians, the width of the interference fringes observed on the screen is $-$\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FENR8onH2W_XtUq7ebdlYxS6lnfGj5tBHA_ydHu2P8ZE.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FENR8onH2W_XtUq7ebdlYxS6lnfGj5tBHA_ydHu2P8ZE.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FENR8onH2W_XtUq7ebdlYxS6lnfGj5tBHA_ydHu2P8ZE.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">","options":["$2.20 \\mathrm{~mm}$","$2.64 \\mathrm{~mm}$","$1.10 \\mathrm{~mm}$","$0.55 \\mathrm{~mm}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FX1xJJoT9OI9QEOPt6GexMuKU73fbd7sqW8TB7Ayz4rY.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FX1xJJoT9OI9QEOPt6GexMuKU73fbd7sqW8TB7Ayz4rY.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FX1xJJoT9OI9QEOPt6GexMuKU73fbd7sqW8TB7Ayz4rY.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\n$\\mathrm{S}$ and $\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{I}}$ are source of $\\mathrm{YDSE}\\theta=0.5 \\times 10^{-3}$ radian (very small)\n\n\\begin{array}{l}\n\\mathrm{D}=\\mathrm{SO} \\cos \\theta+100 \\\\\n=20 \\times 1+100 \\\\\n=120 \\mathrm{~cm} \\\\\n\\mathrm{~d}=2 \\times \\mathrm{SO} \\sin \\theta \\\\\n\\Rightarrow \\quad 2 \\times 20 \\times \\theta \\\\\n\\Rightarrow \\quad 40 \\times 0.5 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{~cm} \\\\\n2 \\times 10^{-2} \\mathrm{~cm} \\\\\n\\beta=\\frac{\\lambda \\mathrm{D}}{\\mathrm{d}}=\\frac{440 \\times 10^{-6} \\times 120 \\times 10^{2}}{2 \\times 10^{-2} \\times 10^{2}} \\\\\n264 \\times 10^{-2} \\\\\n\\Rightarrow 2.64 \\mathrm{~mm}\n\\end{array}\n","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2016 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d21328c7acf306515552"},{"id":52551,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Wave Optics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"One end of a slack wire (Young's modulus Y, length $L$ and cross-sectional area A) is clamped to a rigid wall and the other end to a block (mass m) which rests on a smooth horizontal plane. The block is set in motion with a speed $\\mathrm{v}$. What is the maximum distance the block will travel after the wire becomes taut?","options":["$v \\sqrt{\\frac{mL}{A Y}}$","$v \\sqrt{\\frac{2 mL}{A Y}}$","$v \\sqrt{\\frac{mL}{2 A Y}}$","$L \\sqrt{\\frac{m v}{A Y}}$"],"answer":0,"solution":"$\\frac{1}{2} m v^{2}=\\frac{1}{2} k x^{2}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2015 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213abe6fc304a36955b"},{"id":52552,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Wave Optics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"An unpolarized beam of light of intensity $\\mathrm{I}_{0}$ passes through two linear polarizers making an angle of $30^{\\circ}$ with respect to each other. The emergent beam will have an intensity -","options":["$\\frac{3 \\mathrm{I}_{0}}{4}$","$\\frac{\\sqrt{3} \\mathrm{I}_{0}}{4}$","$\\frac{3 \\mathrm{I}_{0}}{8}$","$\\frac{\\mathrm{I}_{0}}{8}$"],"answer":2,"solution":"$\\mathrm{I}_{0} \\frac{\\text { Firet }}{\\text { Polarizer }} \\mathrm{I}_{0} / 2 \\frac{\\text { Malus law }}{\\text { il polarizer }} \\rightarrow\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{I}_{0}}{2}\\right) \\cos ^{2} 30^{\\circ}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2012 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2136a383f4d42794738"},{"id":52553,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Wave Optics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A plane polarized light passed through successive polarizers which are rotated by $30^{\\circ}$ with respect to each other in the clockwise direction. Neglecting absorption by the polarizers and given that the first polarizer's axis is parallel to the plane of polarization of the incident light, the intensity of light at the exit of the fifth polarizer is closest to -","options":["same as that of the incident light","$17.5 \\%$ of the incident light","$30 \\%$ of the incident light","zero"],"answer":2,"solution":"$\\mathrm{I}=\\mathrm{I}_{0}\\left(\\cos ^{2} \\phi\\right)^{4}=\\mathrm{I}_{0} \\times\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)^{4}=30 \\%$ of $\\mathrm{I}_{0}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2011 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213707d29579313dad9"},{"id":52554,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Wave Optics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"In a Young's double slit experiment the intensity of light at each slit is $\\mathrm{I}_{0}$. Interference pattern in observed along a direction parallel to the line $\\mathrm{S}_{1} \\mathrm{~S}_{2}$, on screen S.-\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FLQwsc53qCJCNKAtPWlFEklpYp-PeiW_SR-HllUyi3xY.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FLQwsc53qCJCNKAtPWlFEklpYp-PeiW_SR-HllUyi3xY.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FLQwsc53qCJCNKAtPWlFEklpYp-PeiW_SR-HllUyi3xY.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\nThe minimum, maximum, and the intensity averaged over the entire screen are respectively","options":["$0,4 \\mathrm{I}_{0}, 2 \\mathrm{I}_{0}$","$0,4 \\mathrm{I}_{0}, \\mathrm{I}_{0}$","$\\mathrm{I}_{0}, 2 \\mathrm{I}_{0}, 3 \\mathrm{I}_{0} / 2$","$0,2_{0}, \\mathrm{I}_{0}$"],"answer":0,"solution":"$\\begin{array}{l}\n\\mathrm{I}_{\\min }=0 \\\\\n\\mathrm{I}_{\\mathrm{max}}=4 \\mathrm{I}_{0} \\\\\n\\mathrm{I}_{\\mathrm{av}}=2 \\mathrm{I}_{0}\n\\end{array}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2012 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213884a2e8b8ab25e7c"},{"id":52555,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Wave Optics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Monochromatic light passes through a prism. Compares to that in air, inside the prism the light's","options":["speed and wavelength are different but frequency remains same.","speed and frequency are different but wavelength remains same.","frequency and wavelength are different but speed remains same.","speed, wavelength and frequency are all different."],"answer":0,"solution":"No Solution","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2014 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213d59a02c9e69cd4a0","questionType":"singleCorrect","type":"singleCorrect"},{"id":52556,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Wave Optics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"In Young's double slit experiment, the distance between the two slits is $0.1 \\mathrm{~mm}$, the distance between the slits and the screen is $1 \\mathrm{~m}$ and the wavelength of the light used is $600 \\mathrm{~nm}$. The intensity at a point on the screen is $75 \\%$ of the maximum intensity. What is the smallest distance of this point from the central fringe?","options":["$1.0 \\mathrm{~mm}$","$2.0 \\mathrm{~mm}$","$0.5 \\mathrm{~mm}$","$1.5 \\mathrm{~mm}$"],"answer":0,"solution":"$\\mathrm{d}=0.1 \\mathrm{~mm}, \\mathrm{D}=1 \\mathrm{~m}, \\lambda=600 \\mathrm{~nm}\\mathrm{I}_{\\mathrm{p}}=75 \\%$ of maximum or $\\mathrm{I}_{\\mathrm{p}}=3 \\mathrm{I}_{0}$\nWhere $I_{0}$ is the intensity of a single wave\nnow $\\mathrm{I}_{\\mathrm{P}}=3 \\mathrm{I}_{0}=\\left(\\sqrt{\\mathrm{I}_{0}}\\right)^{2}+\\left(\\sqrt{\\mathrm{I}_{0}}\\right)^{2}+2 \\sqrt{\\mathrm{I}_{0} \\times \\mathrm{I}_{0}} \\cos \\phi\\therefore \\cos \\phi=\\cos \\frac{\\pi}{3}$, also $\\Delta \\mathrm{x}=\\frac{\\mathrm{yd}}{\\mathrm{D}}$\nnow $\\Delta \\mathrm{x}=\\frac{\\lambda}{2 \\pi} \\times \\frac{\\pi}{3}=\\frac{\\lambda}{6} \\quad \\therefore \\mathrm{y}=\\frac{\\lambda \\mathrm{D}}{6 \\mathrm{~d}}=\\frac{600 \\times 10^{-9} \\times 1}{6 \\times 0.1 \\times 10^{-3}}$ or $\\mathrm{y}=1 \\mathrm{~m}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2010 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213a99bf20b44b0b274"},{"id":52557,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Wave Optics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"In a Young's double slit set-up, light from a laser source falls on a pair of very narrow slits separated by $1.0$ micrometer and bright fringes separated by $1.0$ millimeter are observed on a distant screen. If the frequency of the laser light is doubled, what will be the separation of the bright fringes ?","options":["$0.25 \\mathrm{~mm}$","$0.5 \\mathrm{~mm}$","$1.0 \\mathrm{~mm}$","$2.0 \\mathrm{~mm}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"Separation\n$\\begin{array}{l}\n\\text { Bright fringe }=\\frac{\\lambda D}{d} \\\\\nf \\lambda=c\n\\end{array}\nIf $\\mathrm{f}$ is doubled\n$\\lambda$ become halved\n$\\therefore \\beta$ become half\n$\\beta=\\frac{1}{2}=0.5 \\mathrm{~mm}","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2011 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213c4224372b4df5de8"},{"id":52558,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Wave Optics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A light beam travelling along the $\\mathrm{x}$ axis with planar wavefront is incident on a medium of thickness $\\mathrm{t}$. In the region, where light is falling the refractive index can be taken to be varying such that $\\frac{\\mathrm{dn}}{\\mathrm{dy}}\\gt 0$. The light beam on the other side of the medium will emerge","options":["parallel to the $\\mathrm{x}$-axis","bending downward","bending upward","split into two or more beams"],"answer":2,"solution":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FklN4VP6MXGl4XNDQyp0KG0eS6RsNSTBybblaFgRzl3Q.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FklN4VP6MXGl4XNDQyp0KG0eS6RsNSTBybblaFgRzl3Q.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FklN4VP6MXGl4XNDQyp0KG0eS6RsNSTBybblaFgRzl3Q.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FN1RnhSM8FLLB5pME_e6Ia0o_NB5DMa6tFnJXNH4yKFA.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FN1RnhSM8FLLB5pME_e6Ia0o_NB5DMa6tFnJXNH4yKFA.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FN1RnhSM8FLLB5pME_e6Ia0o_NB5DMa6tFnJXNH4yKFA.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\nIn y direction refractive index is increasing therefore speed of light is decreasing","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (19 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2137100a3183459ffd6"},{"id":52559,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Wave Optics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Which is the following colourful patterns is due to diffraction of light?","options":["Rainbow","White light dispersed using a prism","Colours observed on compact disc","Blue colour of sky"],"answer":2,"solution":"<p>(A) Rainbow occurs because of refraction, reflection & dispersion of light.</p>\n\n<p>(B) Dispersion</p>\n\n<p>(C) Due to presence of concentric grooves in compact disc, light gets diffracted & produced colorful pattern.</p>\n\n<p>(D) Due to scattering of blue color.</p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2020 (SA)","_marksId":"614da50273426027900453af"},{"id":52560,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Wave Optics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"<p>Unpolarised red light is incident on the surface of a lake at incident angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></math>. An observer seeing the light reflected from the water surface through a polariser notices that on rotating the polariser, the intensity of light drops to zero at a certain orientation. The red light is replaced by unpolarised blue light. The observer sees the same effect with reflected blue light at incident angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></math>.</p>\n\n<p>Then,</p>","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo><</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo><</mo><mn>45</mn><mo>°</mo></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>></mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>></mo><mn>45</mn><mo>°</mo></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>></mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>></mo><mn>45</mn><mo>°</mo></math>"],"answer":2,"solution":"<p>As intensity of reflected light changes on rotating the polaroid this means reflected beam is polarised. </p>\n<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252F361626e7-ca0a-4bde-aff5-f2fc09d3235d-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F361626e7-ca0a-4bde-aff5-f2fc09d3235d-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F361626e7-ca0a-4bde-aff5-f2fc09d3235d-image.png?alt=media\"/></p>\n<p>By Brewster's law, </p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>tan</mi><mi>θ</mi></math></p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>Blue</mi><mo> </mo></mrow></msub><mo>></mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>Red</mi><mo> </mo></mrow></msub></math></p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mi>tan</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>></mo><mi>tan</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></math></p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>></mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></math></p>\n<p>Also, from geometry of figure, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>θ</mi><mo>></mo><mn>45</mn><mo>°</mo></math>.</p>\n<p>So, we have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>></mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>></mo><mn>45</mn><mo>°</mo></math>.</p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2019 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da7172a39cd8628203d7a"},{"id":52561,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Wave Optics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"<p>Which of the following is not true about the total lunar eclipse?</p>\n\n<p> </p>","options":["A lunar eclipse can occur on a new moon and full moon day","The lunar eclipse would occur roughly every month, if the orbits of earth and moon were perfectly coplanar","The moon appears red during the eclipse because the blue light is absorbed in earth's atmosphere and red is transmitted","A lunar eclipse can occur only on a full moon day"],"answer":0,"solution":"A lunar eclipse occurs only on a full moon day.<br />\nSo, option (a) is incorrect.","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2018 (SA)","_marksId":"614da405a916143e39ab19eb"}]}